# Agent access to Harbor Ledger

Status: proposed design, ready for implementation review. `docs/brief.md` remains the source of truth for owner decisions; this document supplies design choices and explicitly marked assumptions. No provider configuration or runtime behavior has been changed.

## Decision and security boundary

An agent connection is a delegation by one person to one client installation, approved by an Admin of exactly one firm workspace. Its grant is permanently bound to that workspace. A consultant working for three firms needs three separately approved connections and separate credentials, even when using the same agent application.

Use one canonical remote MCP resource at `https://mcp.<product-domain>/mcp` (domain placeholder). Workspace selection happens during authorization. There is no cross-firm token, workspace-switch tool, or request argument that can redirect an existing grant. Changing the browser's active workspace has no effect on issued credentials. A new workspace requires a new authorization and that firm's Admin approval.

Example: Morgan belongs to Cedar and Elm. Cedar's Admin approves Morgan's desktop-agent connection for reading and annotation. That connection cannot list Elm's workpapers or read or annotate an Elm workpaper by ID. Elm's Admin must approve a separate connection. The agent host must keep their credentials and task contexts separate; if it cannot keep multiple connections to the same endpoint separate, support only one at a time and require disconnect/reconnect rather than broaden a token.

Server isolation cannot prevent a consultant or agent host that legitimately receives both firms' data from copying it between conversations. Document that limitation for firms and require a separate agent task/context per firm; Harbor Ledger never combines the data through a connection.

## Ownership and records

The managed auth provider owns identity, organization membership, roles, OAuth issuance, and credential lifecycle. Each firm workspace maps uniquely to one provider organization. Harbor Ledger owns connection requests, Admin decisions, grant state, resource permissions, and audit records. Do not infer firm identity from email domains, display names, or a consultant's most recently used workspace.

Persist these logical records (physical tables may differ):

| Record | Required facts |
| --- | --- |
| Workspace mapping | Stable workspace ID and unique provider organization ID |
| Connection request | ID, workspace/organization, requesting subject and membership instance, OAuth client ID, installation/connection ID, requested capabilities, creation/expiry times, state |
| Approval | Request ID, exact approved capability set, approving subject and membership, decision time, policy version, optional reason |
| Connection grant | Immutable workspace/organization, subject, membership instance, client and installation identity; approval reference; approved capabilities; creation/expiry times; revocation state/version; provider authorization reference |
| Credential binding | Provider-controlled verified association of each authorization/token family with exactly one grant; rotation must preserve it |
| Audit event | Workspace, actor, connection/request/grant, action, decision, target IDs, timestamp, correlation ID, policy version |

Never attach a generic user token to whichever grant happens to match a caller-supplied ID. The provider must supply a verified grant binding through signed claims or authenticated introspection. A connection ID is an identifier, not a secret or evidence of authority. A software client registration identifies the application, not a separately approved installation; do not share approval across installations or people.

## Connect and approve

1. The agent starts the managed provider's OAuth authorization-code flow with PKCE. Validate registered redirect URIs exactly, protect browser transactions against CSRF, and bind the transaction to client, installation, selected resource, and requested capabilities. Do not use browser session tokens or API keys as MCP credentials.
2. The consultant signs in and explicitly chooses one firm from currently verified memberships. Show the firm name prominently and explain that this connection reaches only that firm. A workspace ID supplied as a hint is validated against membership, never trusted as authorization.
3. Create a connection request for that immutable tuple. Request read access by default; annotation is an explicit additional choice. A request expires after seven days. Client names and icons are presentation only; show verified client identity and requested access to the approver.
4. A current Admin in the selected firm opens its Agent connections page, reviews the person, agent installation, firm, and permissions, then approves a subset or denies. Recheck Admin membership and role server-side at decision time. Approval is an explicit, audited action even when the requester is an Admin; no silent self-approval. For this design, an Admin may explicitly approve their own connection.
5. Pending or denied requests cannot call workpaper tools. Do not keep an OAuth authorization code alive while awaiting an Admin. After approval, the consultant resumes a fresh OAuth transaction that proves the same subject, firm, client, installation, and access set. Consume the approval once when activating its grant; retries converge on the same activation. Changed tuples require a new request.
6. Immediately before issuance, recheck active membership, approval, request expiry, and scope. The provider issues credentials bound to that grant. If provider issuance cannot be delayed until approval, any intermediate credential must be unusable at the MCP resource until activation; never accept it based on identity alone.
7. Label the saved connection with the firm and agent installation. Show requested, pending, approved, denied, expired, and revoked entries to the requester within that firm. The firm Admin can review all of that firm's connections and revoke them. Neither view reveals the consultant's other memberships.

Approval is for the exact connection and capability ceiling. A new installation, person, workspace, or broader capability set needs a new approval. Reauthorization and refresh within the same unexpired grant do not widen it or create a second connection. Revoked or expired grants cannot be revived by signing in again.

## Authorization on every call

All MCP adapters call the same product capabilities as the app. The shared server boundary constructs an authorized context; repositories and mutations require it. Tool visibility, client approval dialogs, prompt instructions, and MCP session IDs are not authorization boundaries.

For every request:

1. Verify the credential with the managed provider's backend integration: signature or introspection, configured issuer, resource/audience binding, validity times, and client identity. Reject ID tokens, wrong-stage credentials, and tokens for other resources. Missing required bindings fail closed.
2. Resolve the immutable grant from verified credential facts. Require matching subject, client, installation, organization, and authorization reference. Require an active, unexpired, unrevoked grant with a committed approval. Do not select another grant when this one fails.
3. Recheck that the workspace and subject are active and the original membership instance is still active in that organization. Read current role and resource permissions rather than treating issuance-time role claims as lasting authority.
4. Authorize the operation using the intersection below. Unknown or empty scopes grant nothing. A later role promotion cannot exceed the Admin-approved ceiling.
5. Apply any plan/usage gate to this grant's firm, then execute the workspace-scoped capability. Bill usage only to that firm. Plan eligibility never substitutes for membership or approval, and a consultant's personal plan cannot unlock a firm.

```text
effective capabilities = current member capabilities
                       ∩ current workpaper permissions
                       ∩ Admin-approved grant capabilities
                       ∩ verified token scopes
```

| Existing MCP tool (schema unchanged) | Required capability / proposed OAuth scope | Workspace enforcement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `list_workpapers { query?, cursor? }` | `workpapers:read` | Filter by grant workspace and member visibility before search, counts, or pagination |
| `read_workpaper { id }` | `workpapers:read` | Resolve by `(grant.workspace_id, id)` and check visibility |
| `annotate_workpaper { id, note }` | `workpapers:annotate` | Resolve by `(grant.workspace_id, id)`; check annotation permission and bind the new note to that workspace and author |

Proposed role baseline: Admin, Preparer, and Reviewer can read and annotate workpapers they may access. Admin alone manages agent approvals. This does not grant agents workspace administration, role changes, workpaper deletion, or final audit sign-off. Existing stricter workpaper rules still apply; confirm the role baseline before implementation.

Keep the three-tool catalog static and authorize each invocation. No `workspace_id` is added to these tools. Reject unexpected scope-selection fields; never interpret a note or query as instructions to change authority. Return the same not-found result for a missing ID and an ID outside the authorized workspace, without another firm's name or metadata.

Scope search indexes, joins, attachments, generated links, caches, deduplication keys, and pagination to the same workspace and visibility rules. Bind opaque cursors to workspace, grant, query, and permission context; reauthorize on every page. A cursor from Cedar cannot paginate Elm. Authenticate every request even on an existing MCP transport session; no process-global active workspace. Never pass provider credentials through to downstream services.

## Revocation, expiry, and concurrent work

Proposed defaults: access tokens live at most ten minutes; grants expire after 90 days; refresh tokens rotate with reuse detection and cannot outlive the grant. Refresh preserves all bindings and scope ceilings. Provider revocation is defense in depth: the product's live grant check must reject an old, otherwise valid token immediately after local revocation commits.

The consultant may disconnect their own connection; any current firm Admin may revoke any connection in that firm. Membership removal, identity disablement, or workspace closure invalidates dependent grants. Removing and re-adding a consultant creates a new membership instance and requires new approval. Role reductions take effect on the next authorization check; role increases cannot widen a grant. An approver leaving later does not erase historical approval or revoke everyone they approved; another firm Admin can revoke those grants.

Use authoritative current provider membership checks, or a provider mechanism with equivalent freshness guarantees; delayed webhooks alone cannot establish current membership. Signed, deduplicated webhooks also revoke local grants and reconcile lifecycle changes. If authoritative status cannot be established, fail closed. No positive authorization cache may extend access beyond a committed local revocation. Verify these guarantees with the chosen provider before enabling MCP.

Serialize grant checks and mutations against local revocation so no annotation commits after its grant has been revoked. Recheck authority immediately before releasing a read result or download credential; discard results if revoked. Already delivered data cannot be recalled. Any future queued work carries the immutable grant, subject, membership, workspace, and target, and rechecks them at execution and result release. Background workers never borrow a service principal to bypass a revoked delegation.

## Audit and failure behavior

Write append-only, access-controlled firm audit events for requests, approvals/denials, activation, expiry, revocation, scope replacement, refresh/security failures, and tool decisions. Preserve who approved exactly which connection and permissions, under which policy, even after membership removal. Successful annotations record both the human subject and agent grant; read/list events record access without copying workpaper contents.

Approval/revocation state changes and their audit events commit together. Annotation and its audit event commit together. Durably record read access before releasing data; if audit persistence fails, deny release. Record denials when possible without revealing foreign targets to a firm's audit view. Logs exclude tokens, authorization codes, refresh secrets, note bodies, query text, and document contents. Firm Admins can inspect and export only their firm's approval and access history.

Return protocol-appropriate authentication challenges for invalid credentials and insufficient scopes, and stable product errors for pending approval, revoked/expired connections, unavailable workpapers, and temporarily unverifiable access. Give a concrete next step such as requesting Admin approval or reconnecting. Do not reveal raw provider errors or suggest changing a workspace argument. No tool call needs a fresh human confirmation once its approved capability is available.

## Assumptions and implementation prerequisites

- Firm contracts in the brief override generic defaults: retain one organization per firm and per-firm billing. Do not auto-create personal workspaces or make a consultant's subscription the access authority.
- The provider is unspecified. This design assumes it can securely bind an OAuth authorization and refresh family to one organization, installation, and Harbor Ledger grant. Verify real issued credentials and refresh behavior before implementation. If it cannot supply the necessary binding, choose a compatible managed integration; do not ship a generic multi-organization token with browser-based workspace selection or silently weaken the requirement. Do not build a custom token issuer as a workaround.
- OAuth details, provider features, MCP revision support, and target-client behavior have not been externally verified for this documentation task. Verify them against the selected provider and supported clients before release; advertise only enforced metadata/scopes and supported flows. Publish protected-resource discovery and use the provider's own authorization-server metadata. Enterprise-managed authorization may be added only with native provider support and the same firm approval and grant checks; it cannot bypass them.
- Read/annotation role permissions, explicit Admin self-approval, seven-day request expiry, ten-minute token lifetime, and 90-day grant expiry are proposed defaults. They permit implementation planning without claiming owner approval. Firms may later need a second approver or shorter expiry; policy tightening must invalidate incompatible grants.
- Approval covers all workpapers the member may access in that firm, including future workpapers. There is no new workpaper allowlist UI in this design. Existing resource restrictions always narrow access.
- Audit retention and export format require a firm contract decision before production. Preserve events without destructive cleanup until a retention policy is approved; no legal retention period is invented here.
- No MCP pricing change, new plan entitlement, provider selection, production domain, or auth configuration is decided by this document. Those implementation choices must preserve this boundary.

## Acceptance scenarios before release

1. A consultant has Cedar and Elm memberships. Cedar approval alone cannot enable Elm access. A forged Elm ID, organization claim, workspace argument, cursor, attachment link, or cached result cannot cross the boundary.
2. Browser workspace switching and simultaneous calls through two approved connections keep each connection bound to its own firm, usage account, audit trail, and data. A client unable to isolate credentials is refused that usage mode.
3. Pending, denied, expired, and consumed requests cannot activate another installation. Non-Admins cannot approve; Admin authority is rechecked at approval. A refresh or reconnect cannot elevate approved read-only access into annotation.
4. Wrong issuer, stage, audience, subject, client, installation, grant binding, or empty/missing scope is rejected. Discovery and client tool caches cannot bypass call authorization.
5. Local revocation blocks existing access tokens and refresh immediately. Membership removal, remove/reinvite, role reduction, workspace closure, and unavailable membership verification all deny the appropriate access. Exercise delayed provider events and revocation racing a read, annotation, refresh, and future job.
6. Audit persistence failure cannot produce an unaudited approval, mutation, or released read. Each firm can reconstruct its Admin approvals and accesses without seeing another firm's membership or data.
